Notebook[{
Cell["ArrayMultiply", "ObjectName",ExpressionUUID->"fd7d5306-7cf6-9946-a975-912ea7d44012"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"ArrayMultiply", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"arr1", ",", " ", "arr2", ",", " ", "..."}], "}"}], ",", " ", 
     "spec"}], "]"}], "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "bfedc24f-34c5-1246-9747-008e69b156df"],
 " ",
 "contracts the given arrays according to the index specification spec, in \
the manner of Einstein summation."
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"8dd7496a-bab7-8b49-b317-a2b7d85c52ec"],

Cell["Details and Options", "NotesSection",ExpressionUUID->"0edbc64c-e115-7e47-bc25-10aecdb84e22"],

Cell["\<\
The specification may be written as a string, \"ij,jk->ik\", in which each \
character is an index label, or as a rule between lists of labels, {{1, 2}, \
{2, 3}} -> {1, 3}.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"d93554a3-4158-9b47-b633-55d904cccb63"],

Cell["\<\
Indices named in the output part survive; every other index is summed over. \
With the output part omitted, the indices that occur exactly once survive, in \
sorted order, and the rest are summed.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"79dfa3cd-f85e-ce47-b719-89b276ace27c"],

Cell["\<\
An index may occur in any number of the arrays, and may occur in the output \
as well as in the inputs. That covers diagonals and three-way contractions, \
which a pairwise contraction could not express.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"e272f3e5-302d-354b-8eb5-c0fc43e01ad3"],

Cell["\<\
The arrays may be given as array objects or as plain nested lists, and an \
array object comes through as its entries. The result is an ArrayObject \
whatever went in.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"1aa1b6a9-5251-f142-8612-ad961d24454a"],

Cell["\<\
Contracting every index away leaves a single entry, which comes back as the \
0-array: an ArrayObject with no indices, whose Normal is the scalar itself.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"62b4663a-4682-4a4a-98a8-30be97b84dc6"],

Cell["\<\
An array of order zero is named by an empty group of labels, so that \
\",ij->ij\" multiplies a matrix by a scalar. The output part may equally be \
written empty, \"ij,ji->\", to ask for every index to be contracted away.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"2af326b8-7833-b548-a6e2-6f990bce4bb0"],

Cell["\<\
Arrays with numeric entries and arrays with symbolic ones may be contracted \
together: the entries of the result are simply the expressions the \
contraction builds, and there is only one kind of array for it to come back \
as.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"3153d3a0-6ea2-ca4b-acbf-d90c63eb5a28"],

Cell["\<\
The number of labels given for an array must equal its number of indices, and \
every occurrence of a label must have the same length, or the corresponding \
message is produced and $Failed returned.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"554224d0-a494-7945-9b00-ba65ffeb8bee"],

Cell["Examples", "PrimaryExamplesSection",ExpressionUUID->"975ec06f-1a17-9741-84e6-903b3a926224"],

Cell["The ordinary matrix product:", "ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"67d89081-53d4-2a46-a527-e79a65a1fd1a"],

Cell["\<\
ArrayMultiply[{{{1, 2}, {3, 4}}, {{0, 1}, {1, 0}}}, \"ij,jk->ik\"]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"4c121646-e374-604e-95d1-8f1132a6648a"],

